// ==========================================================================
// PostHog, on our own origin.
//
// THREE THINGS MAKE THIS DIFFERENT FROM THE STOCK SNIPPET, and each one is
// here for a reason worth keeping:
//
// 1. cookieless_mode: 'always'. Nothing is written to the reader's machine:
//    no cookie, no localStorage, no sessionStorage, no IndexedDB. That is what
//    makes a consent banner unnecessary rather than merely unfashionable,
//    because what ePrivacy asks permission for is the storage, not the
//    counting. A landing page that opens with a consent dialog is one that
//    argues with you before it has said what it is. Instead PostHog's server
//    hashes address + user agent + host with a salt it rotates daily, so a
//    reader is one visitor across pages for a day and a stranger tomorrow. It
//    needs "cookieless tracking" on in the project's settings, or every event
//    is discarded on arrival.
//
//    **Watch the network, not the config.** On posthog-js 1.415.1 this mode
//    silently sent nothing (consent was recorded in the storage the mode
//    disables and stuck at 'pending'), so the site ran on persistence:
//    'memory' until 2026-11-11, when 1.447.10 was checked sending every event
//    with nothing stored. has_opted_out_capturing() still reads true and
//    consent still reads 'pending' there; is_capturing() is what tells the
//    truth. If you touch this, look for a POST to /ph/i/v0/e/.
//
//    Location is the proxy's job, not PostHog's: see worker/index.ts.
//
// 2. api_host points at this origin. worker/index.ts forwards /ph to PostHog.
//    Built from location.origin rather than hardcoded so a preview build, a
//    wrangler dev run and production each talk to their own proxy instead of
//    every one of them reporting into production's.
//
// 3. autocapture is off. Three routes and four interactions worth naming: a
//    firehose of every click on the page would cost bandwidth on the reader's
//    machine to produce data nobody is going to read. The events that matter
//    are declared in the markup (see below) where you can see them.
//
// THE KEY IS IN THE FILE ON PURPOSE. A phc_ token is write-only: it can send
// events and read nothing back, which is why PostHog ships it inside client
// bundles. Putting it in .env instead would buy no secrecy and would cost a
// silent failure, because release.sh redeploys this site at the end of every
// publish and a machine missing the file would quietly ship a site that
// reports nothing. The key that CAN read data is a personal API key, and that
// one is not here and never should be.
//
// DEV IS SILENT, production is not, and the switch is the build rather than a
// flag anyone has to remember. Note that `npm run cf-preview` is a production
// build, so it does report: that is what makes it possible to check this works
// before deploying, and it is worth knowing before you click around in it.
//
// ---- how the events below are declared -----------------------------------
// One delegated listener for the whole site. An element opts in by carrying
// data-ph="event_name", and any data-ph-* attribute beside it becomes a
// property (data-ph-to-version arrives as to_version):
//
//     <a class="download" data-ph="download_clicked" data-ph-location="hero">
//
// Markup rather than per-page handlers, because the alternative is a script
// block on every page repeating the same querySelector plumbing, and because
// an attribute sits where you can see it while you are editing the markup it
// belongs to. The listener runs in the capture phase so it still fires on an
// anchor that is about to navigate away.
//
// data-ph-demo is the one exception, and it marks the app window on the
// landing page: the first click inside it reports demo_engaged, once per page
// load. The window is the most expensive thing on the site and nothing
// currently says whether anyone touches it. It is an attribute rather than the
// obvious `.app-frame` selector because the changelog page uses that class for
// its preview window, and dragging a release note around is not engaging with
// the demo.
//
// ---- and why the comments stop here --------------------------------------
// Everything above is build-time only. The script below ships to every reader
// on every page, so it carries the reasoning it needs and no more.
// ==========================================================================

const KEY = 'phc_s9AQUzSKYsbdi8aoCWkaedPK2Cbjr9KEmYJqjgWa89oa';
const ENABLED = import.meta.env.PROD;
---

{ENABLED && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ KEY }}>
